    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d896">

                <pb facs="0840" n="832" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg832"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/2358: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-11-13T00:00:00-05:00">November 13,
                        1940—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>473. Reference Department’s 455, November 7, 10 p.m.,<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d896-f1"/> and Shanghai’s 1216, November 7, 7 p.m. If you have not
                    already made the approach requested in the Department’s 455, November 7, 10
                    p.m., please change the expression of hope in the last sentence of paragraph
                    numbered 1 therein to an expression of regret and disappointment on the part of
                    this Government. If you have already made the approach in question, please
                    supplement it by saying that this Government is disappointed and regretful that
                    the Japanese Government should have seen fit to undertake alteration of the
                    status of the Chinese courts in the French Concession at Shanghai without the
                    consent of the Chinese Government at Chungking and add that the Japanese
                    Government will of course appreciate that its action in this respect tends to
                        <pb facs="0841" n="833" xml:id="pg833"/> aggravate problems in the relations
                    of the United States and Japan.<anchor n="69" xml:id="d896-f2"/></p>
                <p>Sent to Tokyo via Shanghai. Repeated to Chungking, Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d896-f1" n="68" xml:id="d896-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was similar to the Department’s telegram No. 694. November 7, 6
                    p.m., to the Chargé in France, <ref target="#pg828">p. 828</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d896-f2" n="69" xml:id="d896-f2-content">
                <p>For action by the Embassy in Japan, see despatch No. 5158, November 20, from the
                    Ambassador in Japan, <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_892"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 892</ref>; for the Japanese reply on
                    December 18, see <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_893"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >ibid.</hi>, p. 893</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
